## Layout described in text → confirm before coding (LAW10, visual form)

When the work is positioning elements relative to each other (X left of Y, row 1 vs
row 2, this spans 2 columns) and the description is textual, coding straight from prose
is fragile — arrows like `<--->` and left/right invert easily. Sketch the layout back as
ASCII (or a quick render) and confirm it BEFORE coding if there is any ambiguity. **A 2nd
misunderstanding of the same layout = STOP and sketch**, don't guess a 3rd arrangement.
This is the layout counterpart of the attempt-counter: a repeated miss is the trip.
(Distinct from open design decisions, where you show visual options and let the user
choose — here the user already described the layout; you are confirming your reading of it.)

## Order for new pages/components

1. Short proposal (what + why — not the full layout).
2. **Build a first render and show it to the user** before writing the third
   document. For visual work, seeing the screen beats the third doc — the user
   corrects the layout in seconds by looking, and that correction invalidates part
   of what was written ahead of it.
3. Agree, then complete the proposal/plan with what was learned.
4. `ui-reviewer` in `review` mode (it reads — no build needed) **before** the
   verification step, not after: running it after forces a second verify cycle.
5. `verification-before-completion` once, at the end, with the reviewer's notes
   already folded in.

## Agents

- **ui-reviewer** — invoke when creating new pages or modifying existing UI.
  `review` (report only) runs *before* verification; `fix` (report + corrections)
  when you want it to apply them.
- **doc-updater** — invoke if changes are multi-area.

## After modifying, update

Update frontend area doc in the same act if the change is architectural.
